# AULA F75. The keyboard presents two entirely different USB identities and
# both need access, because which one is live depends on how it is connected:
#
#   3554:fa09  Compx 2.4G receiver  - wireless. Battery over the vendor pipe.
#   258a:010c  "BY Tech Gaming Keyboard" - what the keyboard itself enumerates
#              as over USB-C. Plugging the cable in takes the radio down, so
#              this is the only device present in wired mode.
#
# Both hidraw nodes are root-only by default, so the helper cannot open them.
# uaccess hands an ACL to whoever is logged in at the active seat, which is
# narrower than the MODE="0666" that older guides suggest - that would expose
# the keyboard's raw input stream, keystrokes included, to every account on
# the machine.
#
# The 60- prefix matters. udev runs rules in lexical order and systemd's
# /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-uaccess.rules is what turns the tag into an ACL, so
# a file numbered above 70 sets the tag too late and silently does nothing.
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="3554", ATTRS{idProduct}=="fa09", TAG+="uaccess"
KERNEL=="hidraw*", SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="258a", ATTRS{idProduct}=="010c", TAG+="uaccess"
